(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-inflammatory-lung-disease-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Inflammatory Lung Diseases Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In the workup of interstitial lung disease, what does a high antinuclear antibody level point to?","choices":["Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Active sarcoidosis","Eosinophilic pneumonia","Collagen vascular disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Among the laboratory findings in interstitial lung disease, a high antinuclear antibody level points to the collagen vascular diseases, the autoimmune connective tissue group that includes lupus, rheumatoid arthritis and systemic sclerosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 18, 25, 44"},{"q":"Which laboratory test is useful in silicosis?","choices":["Serum antibodies to the agent","Arterial blood gas","Antinuclear antibody titer","Cytoplasmic antineutrophil antibody"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In silicosis the laboratory workup is an arterial blood gas; otherwise labs are not contributory. Pulmonary function shows reduced forced vital capacity and forced expiratory volume in 1 second, and lavage returns thick, milky fluid with foamy macrophages. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 25, 62, 69"},{"q":"Which sarcoidosis finding is the stated exception among the cutaneous indications for oral corticosteroids?","choices":["Erythema nodosum","Lupus pernio","Lesions on the back and arms","Parotid gland enlargement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cutaneous lesions other than erythema nodosum are an indication for oral corticosteroids, so erythema nodosum, the itchy and painful rash on the lower legs and ankles, is the exception. Lupus pernio and other skin lesions still count toward treatment. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 43, 47"},{"q":"After microscopic lung injury, what leads to pulmonary fibrosis rather than lung homeostasis?","choices":["The type of stimulus","A single large injury","Aberrant wound healing","Intact wound healing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Exogenous and endogenous stimuli cause microscopic lung injury, separated spatially and temporally. When wound healing is intact the lung returns to homeostasis; when it is aberrant, with genetic predisposition and autoimmune conditions acting on it, the result is pulmonary fibrosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What is the most common chest radiograph finding in asbestosis?","choices":["Pleural plaques","Eggshell hilar calcification","Bilateral hilar adenopathy","Upper-lobe coal macules"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Imaging in asbestosis is often normal early, and pleural plaques are the most common chest radiograph finding, appearing with linear streaking or opacities at the bases; reticulonodular opacities may be seen there, and honeycombing appears in advanced disease. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 44, 53, 58, 63"},{"q":"Which disease is listed among the connective tissue causes of interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Sarcoidosis","Silicosis","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Systemic sclerosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The connective tissue diseases that cause interstitial lung disease, with an autoimmune inflammatory and fibrotic response, include systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis, ankylosing spondylitis, systemic sclerosis, Sjögren's syndrome and polymyositis-dermatomyositis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 18"},{"q":"A ratio of arterial oxygen pressure to fraction of inspired oxygen of 150 mm Hg places acute respiratory distress syndrome in which severity class?","choices":["Outside the criterion","Mild","Moderate","Severe"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The oxygenation severity classes use the ratio of arterial oxygen pressure to fraction of inspired oxygen: mild 200 to 300 mm Hg, moderate 100 to 200 mm Hg, severe under 100 mm Hg. A ratio of 150 falls in the moderate band, where early prone positioning decreases mortality. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 73, 74"},{"q":"Over what period does pulmonary function generally recover after acute respiratory distress syndrome?","choices":["1 to 2 months","6 to 12 months","18 to 24 months","36 to 48 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary function improves over 6 to 12 months, generally recovering within about a year, though functional deficits often remain. Survivors need monitoring for residual diffusion deficits and treatment of post-intensive care muscle wasting, weakness and fatigue. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 72, 74"},{"q":"What do macrophages, lymphocytes, monocytes and neutrophils accumulating in the alveolar walls first produce?","choices":["Alveolitis","Collagen deposition","Honeycombing","Granulomas"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the pathophysiology of fibrosis, macrophages, lymphocytes, monocytes and neutrophils accumulate in the alveolar walls and alveolitis occurs. Alveolitis then leads to fibroblast production, and the collagen those fibroblasts produce causes the fibrosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 6, 20, 26"},{"q":"What is mesothelioma?","choices":["A scar of the terminal bronchioles","An infection of the pleural space","A cancer of the pleural lining","A benign pleural plaque"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mesothelioma is primarily a pleural cancer, a cancer of the pleural lining, and is nearly always caused by asbestos exposure, whose fibers can penetrate further into the lung. Patients frequently develop pleural effusions. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 56, 58, 59"},{"q":"Pneumoconiosis is a chronic fibrotic lung disease caused by inhaling which material?","choices":["Coal or other inorganic dusts","Organic dusts such as moldy hay","Toxic gases such as chlorine","Medications such as amiodarone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pneumoconiosis is a chronic fibrotic lung disease caused by the inhalation of coal dust or various inorganic or silicate dusts. Coal workers' pneumoconiosis, asbestosis and silicosis all come from these inorganic exposures. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 22, 50, 65, 72"},{"q":"How does the hypoxemia of acute respiratory distress syndrome respond to supplemental oxygen?","choices":["It corrects with low-flow oxygen","It appears after each exposure","It waits for fibrosis to form","It is refractory to it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute respiratory distress syndrome causes marked hypoxemia refractory to supplemental oxygen therapy. Epithelial barrier destruction leads to surfactant loss and alveolar collapse, and the refractory hypoxemia follows. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 72, 73"},{"q":"Which imaging finding is characteristic of silicosis?","choices":["Patchy pleural-based honeycombing","Normal heart with air bronchograms","Eggshell calcification of hilar nodes","Pleural plaques at the bases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Silicosis imaging shows nodular opacities throughout, silicotic nodules of 1 to 10 mm forming nodular pulmonary fibrosis, and eggshell calcification of the periphery of the hilar lymph nodes. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 34, 58, 63, 73"},{"q":"Which test helps exclude cardiogenic pulmonary edema when acute respiratory distress syndrome is suspected?","choices":["Echocardiography","Pulmonary artery catheter","Bronchoalveolar lavage","Pulmonary function testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Echocardiographic assessment evaluates cardiac function to exclude hydrostatic cardiogenic pulmonary edema, the main differential. Supporting this, the heart size is normal in acute respiratory distress syndrome, with minimal or absent pleural effusions. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 29, 62, 73, 74"},{"q":"Which group makes up the most common interstitial lung diseases of known etiology?","choices":["Connective tissue diseases","Idiopathic interstitial pneumonias","Infections of the lung","Occupational or environmental exposures"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The most common interstitial lung diseases of known etiology come from occupational or environmental exposures: inorganic dusts, organic dusts, and fumes or gases. When a known cause is suspected, the exposure history at work and in the environment is where it is found. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 13"},{"q":"About how long after asbestos exposure do patients with asbestosis usually seek medical attention?","choices":["1 year","5 years","15 years","30 years"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The asbestos history spans 10 to 20 years, and patients usually seek medical attention about 15 years after exposure. Imaging is often normal early in the disease, so the long gap and the occupational history point to the diagnosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"When is biopsy essential in suspected sarcoidosis?","choices":["When lymphoma remains possible","When hypercalcemia is present","When the film shows stage I","When erythema nodosum appears"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy is essential whenever clinical and radiographic findings suggest the possibility of an alternative diagnosis such as lymphoma. Transbronchial lung biopsy or fine-needle node biopsy confirms sarcoidosis by showing noncaseating granulomas. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 43, 44, 45, 47"},{"q":"When does profound dyspnea typically develop after the initiating insult in acute respiratory distress syndrome?","choices":["Within minutes","12 to 48 hours","7 to 14 days","6 to 12 months"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Profound dyspnea develops 12 to 48 hours after the initiating insult, with tachypnea, labored breathing, intercostal retractions and crackles, and marked hypoxemia refractory to supplemental oxygen. The syndrome must develop within 7 days of a known insult. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 73, 74"},{"q":"Which intervention offers no clinical benefit when used routinely in acute respiratory distress syndrome?","choices":["Early prone positioning","Pulmonary artery catheter","Conservative fluid protocol","Low tidal volume ventilation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Routine pulmonary artery catheters offer no clinical benefit in acute respiratory distress syndrome and are avoided. Cardiac function is assessed instead with echocardiography to exclude hydrostatic cardiogenic pulmonary edema. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 73, 74"},{"q":"Which laboratory result is elevated in 40 to 80% of patients with sarcoidosis?","choices":["Angiotensin-converting enzyme","Antinuclear antibody titer","Cytoplasmic antineutrophil antibody","Basement membrane antibodies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Angiotensin-converting enzyme levels are elevated in 40 to 80% of patients with sarcoidosis. Other laboratory findings include leukopenia, eosinophilia, an elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate, hypercalcemia in about 5% of patients and hypercalciuria. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 25, 44"},{"q":"What is the outlook in hypersensitivity pneumonitis that is caught early?","choices":["Irreversible from onset","Improves despite exposure","Incurable with poor survival","Symptoms can be reversible"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prompt diagnosis is key to removing the patient from the irritating environment: symptoms can be reversible if the disease is caught early, while continued exposure may lead to a worsened disease state, with subacute and chronic fibrotic changes. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 59, 65, 69"},{"q":"In which patients can idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis be diagnosed with 90% confidence without surgical lung biopsy?","choices":["Any age with honeycombing","Over 65 with four classic features","Over 65 with crackles alone","Under 40 with a smoking history"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In patients over 65 years of age, idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis can be diagnosed with 90% confidence when there is idiopathic disease with inspiratory crackles, restrictive physiology, radiographic progressive fibrosis over several years, and diffuse patchy fibrosis with pleural-based honeycombing on high-resolution computed tomography. Such patients do not need surgical lung biopsy. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 35"},{"q":"What is the first-line treatment for sarcoidosis that needs therapy?","choices":["Methotrexate","Infliximab","Lung transplant","Corticosteroids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. About 90% of sarcoidosis cases are responsive to corticosteroids and can be controlled with moderate maintenance doses. Oral corticosteroids are indicated for disabling constitutional symptoms, hypercalcemia, eye, heart, nervous system or liver involvement, arthritis, cutaneous lesions other than erythema nodosum and progressive pulmonary lesions. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 46, 47, 54, 71"},{"q":"Which is the most common subtype of idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia?","choices":["Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia","Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia","Acute interstitial pneumonia","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis is the most common subtype of idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia, which is itself the most common diagnosis among patients with interstitial lung disease. Treatment plan and prognosis depend on the subtype diagnosed. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 32, 35, 38"},{"q":"Which reticular pattern on chest radiograph is an early finding in interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Coarse, cystic spaces","Reticulonodular overlap","Fine, ground-glass","Medium, honeycombing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Reticular, curvilinear opacities are graded fine, medium and coarse: the fine, ground-glass pattern is an early finding, medium honeycombing is a late finding, and coarse opacities are cystic spaces. Chest films also show low lung volumes with patchy opacities. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Amiodarone, nitrofurantoin, methotrexate and radiation fall under which class of interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Unknown cause, granulomatous","Known cause, treatment related","Known cause, occupational","Unknown cause, idiopathic"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In the classification of interstitial lung disease, the exposure branch of known causes holds a treatment-related box listing radiation, methotrexate, amiodarone, nitrofurantoin and chemotherapeutics. Chemotherapeutic agents, amiodarone and nitrofurantoin also cause drug-induced pulmonary toxicity. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 72"},{"q":"Which symptom pattern fits idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia?","choices":["Painful shin rash and parotid swelling","Insidious dry cough and exertional dyspnea","Fever and chills hours after exposure","Dyspnea 12 to 48 hours after an insult"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia causes an insidious dry cough, exertional dyspnea and constitutional symptoms such as fatigue and malaise. Examination shows clubbing, bibasilar inspiratory crackles, tachypnea and reduced chest expansion. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 33, 43, 66, 73"},{"q":"In the laboratory workup of interstitial lung disease, what does an increased erythrocyte sedimentation rate point to?","choices":["Goodpasture syndrome","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Active sarcoidosis","Collagen vascular disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Among the laboratory clues in interstitial lung disease, an increased erythrocyte sedimentation rate points to active sarcoidosis, which can also show eosinophilia and, in some patients, an elevated angiotensin-converting enzyme level and hypercalcemia. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 25, 44"},{"q":"What is the most common presenting symptom of interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Shortness of breath","Productive cough","Hemoptysis","Chest pain"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fibrosis makes the lung tissue stiff and restrictive, no longer expanding as it once did, which makes breathing harder; patients most commonly present with shortness of breath, typically progressive exertional dyspnea, sometimes with a persistent nonproductive cough. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 23"},{"q":"In which setting should idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia be suspected?","choices":["Bilateral hilar adenopathy alone","Unexplained interstitial lung disease","Known heavy asbestos exposure","Serum antibodies to an inhaled agent"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia should be suspected in a patient with unexplained interstitial lung disease; it is the most common diagnosis among patients with interstitial lung disease, and when no associated cause is evident the pattern is idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 35, 45, 55, 69"}]};
